  "summary": "I reviewed Harold Vance's active medications against RBC-PHARM-07. Two doses changed: apixaban goes up to 5 mg twice daily, and gabapentin goes down to 600 mg twice daily. Metformin stays the same. A signed progress note citing the policy is in the chart (doc-t0003).\n\n**Values used**\n- **Age:** 82.\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL from 10/20. A 1.88 result dated 10/21 is marked entered in error: the specimen was mislabelled and belongs to another patient. I didn't use it.\n- **Weight:** 143 lb (64.9 kg) from 10/20.\n\n**Changes**\n- **Apixaban 2.5 mg → 5 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0001):** He meets only 1 of the 3 reduction criteria: age 80 or older. His weight is now over 60 kg and his creatinine is under 1.5. He was reduced to 2.5 mg at his February discharge, when he met all 3 criteria during his kidney injury. The discharge summary and your May note both asked for the dose to be reassessed once he recovered.\n- **Gabapentin 600 mg three times daily → 600 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0002):** His estimated creatinine clearance is 43 mL/min. At that level the policy allows 400–1400 mg a day, given twice daily, and he was taking 1800 mg. 1200 mg is the highest dose in range using his current 600 mg tablet. His nerve pain was controlled on the old dose, so it's worth watching for it coming back.\n\n**No change**\n- **Metformin 1000 mg twice daily:** eGFR is 59.8, above the policy's cutoff of 45.\n- **Other medications** (atorvastatin, metoprolol, amlodipine, acetaminophen, nitroglycerin) aren't covered by the policy, so I left them alone.\n\nI didn't message the patient or the pharmacy. Someone should tell him about both dose changes and make sure the pharmacy fills the new strengths.",
